{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["And the meaning of <b>et ha-alah ha-zot</b>: \"Cursed be the man who makes a carved or cast image.\"" "<b>And bless himself in his heart.</b> R. Judah HaLevi, z\"l, said that ve-hitbarekh is as it sounds: when he hears the curse, he blesses in his heart, and he says, \"I shall have peace.\" Like one who, upon hearing the ban, says: it does not apply to me." "<b>Le-ma'an safot.</b> R. Judah HaLevi, z\"l, said it is a term of cutting off, like ha-af tispeh. That is, the one who blesses himself thinks to nullify the words of the righteous man, who is designated by the word רוה together with the word צמאה. He designated the righteous man by רוה, like \"a tree planted by waters,\" and the wicked man by צמאה, like \"a shrub in the desert.\" And he thinks that even the blessing in his heart will interrupt the curse. His interpretation is fine, were it not that the word רוה is an attribute of a noun, and it should have been רוי את הצמא. R. Jonah the Grammarian said that its meaning is: because he will see that the righteous perish there with the wicked, therefore he thinks evil in his heart. This is not correct, because of \"I shall have peace.\" In my opinion, the word safot is a term of addition, like safot ḥatat. And the meaning is: \"I shall have peace\" even though I walk in the stubbornness of my heart, for through the righteousness of the righteous I shall live, for they are many and I am a lone sinner. Therefore it is written after it: lo yoveh HaShem soloaḥ lo, ki az, and the meaning is immediately, this one too will be cut off. This verse is a lasting proof for my interpretation in the word ve-nislaḥ lo. And so safot means that the רוה adds upon the צמאה, and the metaphor of the righteous as רוה is like the meaning of \"a tree planted by streams of water,\" and the wicked as צמאה like the meaning of \"a shrub in the desert.\" And the proof is that it is written at the beginning \"a root bearing wormwood and gall,\" and it is in the place of thirst."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {2 "Reading לעד as לְעֵד ('a proof/witness'), following the common medieval interchange of ayin/alef. The phrase מלת ונסלח לו apparently refers to Ibn Ezra's interpretation of a different verse, but the citation is left deliberately terse as in the original."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:25:33.597934580Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}